
{"id":"d5090752-6952-4584-9acb-b68c9e0451d1","object":"chat.completion","created":1784885134,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\\\\n        raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"case must have 'nodes' and 'required_symbols'\\\\\\\")\\\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or len(nodes) == 0:\\\\n        raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"nodes must be non-empty list\\\\\\\")\\\\n    required_node_fields = ['id','parent','symbol','depth','removable']\\\\n    for n in nodes:\\\\n        for f in required_node_fields:\\\\n            if f not in n:\\\\n                raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"node missing field \\\\\\\" + f)\\\\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\\\n    roots = [n for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None]\\\\n    if len(roots) != 1:\\\\n        raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"must have exactly one root\\\\\\\")\\\\n    root_id = roots[0]['id']\\\\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_by_id}\\\\n    for n in nodes:\\\\n        pid = n['parent']\\\\n        if pid is not None:\\\\n            if pid not in node_by_id:\\\\n                raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"parent \\\\\\\" + pid + \\\\\\\" not found\\\\\\\")\\\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\\\n    subtree_node_ids = {}\\\\n    def dfs_subtree(nid):\\\\n        members = {nid}\\\\n        for c in children[nid]:\\\\n            members.update(dfs_subtree(c))\\\\n        subtree_node_ids[nid] = members\\\\n        return members\\\\n    dfs_subtree(root_id)\\\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\\\n    total_counts = {}\\\\n    for n in nodes:\\\\n        s = n['symbol']\\\\n        total_counts[s] = total_counts.get(s, 0) + 1\\\\n    for s in required_symbols:\\\\n        if s not in total_counts:\\\\n            raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"required symbol \\\\\\\" + s + \\\\\\\" not present\\\\\\\")\\\\n    removed = []\\\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\\\n        nid = node['id']\\\\n        if nid not in retained:\\\\n            continue\\\\n        if not node['removable']:\\\\n            continue\\\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\\\n            continue\\\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\\\n            continue\\\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\\\\n        if has_retained_child:\\\\n            continue\\\\n        subtree_set = subtree_node_ids[nid]\\\\n        retained_subset = subtree_set & retained\\\\n        if not retained_subset:\\\\n            continue\\\\n        sub_counts = {}\\\\n        for rid in retained_subset:\\\\n            s = node_by_id[rid]['symbol']\\\\n            sub_counts[s] = sub_counts.get(s, 0) + 1\\\\n        can_remove = True\\\\n        for s in required_symbols:\\\\n            if total_counts.get(s, 0) - sub_counts.get(s, 0) == 0:\\\\n                can_remove = False\\\\n                break\\\\n        if not can_remove:\\\\n            continue\\\\n        removal_list = sorted(retained_subset, key=lambda rid: (-node_by_id[rid]['depth'], rid))\\\\n        for rid in removal_list:\\\\n            removed.append(rid)\\\\n            retained.remove(rid)\\\\n            s = node_by_id[rid]['symbol']\\\\n            total_counts[s] -= 1\\\\n            if total_counts[s] == 0:\\\\n                del total_counts[s]\\\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\\\n    return {\\\\\\\"removed_node_ids\\\\\\\": removed, \\\\\\\"retained_node_ids\\\\\\\": retained_sorted}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write Python code for solve(case) that implements a grammar-guided node reduction algorithm. The input case has nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. The output must have removed_node_ids (list of strings) and retained_node_ids (list of strings). The algorithm: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" The description: \"Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar; parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded.\" It says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" and \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Also \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we need to iterate over nodes in order of decreasing depth (deepest first). For each node, if it is removable (removable==True) and not root, not a required symbol (check its symbol not in required_symbols? Actually required_symbols are symbols that must be present. The node's symbol is not necessarily the required symbol; but we need to ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still present somewhere in the tree. So we must check if removing this node would cause any required symbol to disappear. Also we cannot remove a node that has any retained children (i.e., if we remove a node, we also remove its subtree? The description says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\". That suggests that if we decide to keep some child, we cannot remove the parent. Actually \"retained children\" likely means children that are not removed. So we cannot remove a node that has any child that is retained (i.e., not removed). So when we consider removing a node, we must ensure that all its children are also being removed (or never existed). But the algorithm seems to process nodes in order, and we might remove a node only if all its descendants are also removed? The typical Perses algorithm: remove a node if its removal does not affect grammar validity and the resulting tree is still interesting. Here we don't have grammar validity or interestingness; we have required_symbols and node removability. So the algorithm: iterate nodes from deepest to shallowest. For each removable node, tentatively remove it. Check if after removal (i.e., removing that node and its entire subtree? Actually if we remove a node, we remove the entire subtree rooted at that node because the node's children are part of it. So we need to check if any required symbol is represented in that subtree. If the subtree contains a required symbol that is not present elsewhere, we cannot remove. Also if the node has children that are already retained (i.e., not removed), we cannot remove the parent because the parent would have retained children. But in this algorithm, children are processed before parents (deeper first). So at the time we consider a parent, some of its children may have already been removed or retained. If a child is retained (i.e., not removed), then the parent cannot be removed because it would lose that retained child. So the condition \"node with retained children\" means if any child is still present (not removed), then cannot remove parent.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Build a mapping from node id to node dict.\n- Build parent-child relationships.\n- Initially, assume all nodes are retained (to be decided). We'll have a set of retained_node_ids.\n- Process nodes sorted by depth descending, and for equal depth, by id ascending (since ID is tie-breaker).\n- For each node:\n  - If node is not removable, skip.\n  - If node is root (id==\"root\" maybe?), skip. But root has removable=False anyway.\n  - If node's symbol is in required_symbols? Actually we never remove a node that is a required symbol. But the spec says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So check if node's symbol in required_symbols, then cannot remove.\n  - Check if any child of this node is already in retained_node_ids. If yes, cannot remove.\n  - Simulate removal: the node and its entire subtree would be gone. We need to check if all required symbols are still present in the tree after removal. That means for each required symbol, there must exist at least one node (other than those in the subtree) that has that symbol. So we need to know the set of symbols present in the subtree of this node. We can precompute for each node the set of symbols in its subtree (including itself). Then after removal, the remaining symbols are the symbols from nodes not in the subtree. So we need that for every s in required_symbols, s is present in the set of symbols of nodes not in the subtree of the node. If that holds, then we can remove.\n  - If all conditions satisfied, then we remove: add node.id to removed_node_ids, remove it from retained (it wasn't added yet). Also we need to remove all descendants? Actually if we remove the node, all descendants are also removed. So we should mark that the entire subtree is removed. But since we process deeper first, we might have already decided to retain some descendant? But if we remove the parent, those descendants must also be removed. So we need to ensure that we don't retain a descendant if its ancestor is removed. One approach: process nodes in decreasing depth; for each node, if it is removable and conditions met, remove it. But if a descendant was already considered removable but not removed (maybe because conditions failed), it could be retained. But if we later remove the ancestor, the descendant must be removed as well. However the algorithm as described in Perses typically works bottom-up: you try to remove a node only if it is \"candidate\" and you check if after removal the tree is still interesting. If you remove, that means the entire subtree is gone. Therefore, if a parent is removed, all children are implicitly removed regardless of their individual removability. So in our processing, when we consider a parent, we should check if any child is retained (i.e., not removed yet). But if we later remove the parent, we must also remove those children. So the algorithm must ensure that when removing a parent, we also remove any children that were previously retained. That suggests we should not pre-decide child retention; instead, we decide removal of a node and that automatically removes its entire subtree. So we should not have a separate decision for each child; the entire subtree's retention depends on the deepest ancestor that remains. So a better approach: Use a set of \"to_remove\" nodes. Process nodes in decreasing depth. For each node, if removable and conditions met, add to to_remove and mark that all descendants are removed (but we don't need to explicitly process them because they are deeper and will be skipped if already removed). However, we need to ensure that when checking conditions for a node, we consider that its children are not yet removed (since we haven't processed them? Actually we process deeper first, so children are processed before parent. So when processing a child, we might decide to remove it if conditions hold. Then when processing parent, if child was removed, then parent's children are not retained. So the condition \"node with retained children\" means that if any child is still present (not removed), then parent cannot remove. If child was removed, it's not retained. So the algorithm naturally works if we decide removals bottom-up; removing a child means it's gone, and parent later sees that child is gone. If we remove a child, that's fine. If we later remove the parent, the entire subtree including the removed child is gone, but the child was already removed anyway. So no conflict. But what if we decide to remove a parent but previously decided to keep a child (i.e., child was not removed)? That would violate the \"no retained children\" condition, so parent cannot be removed. So the condition prevents that.\n\nThus the algorithm: \n- Build tree.\n- Determine for each node the set of symbols in its subtree (including itself). This can be computed via DFS.\n- Initialize retained_node_ids = set of all node ids (initially all are retained).\n- Initialize removed_node_ids = [] (order of removal).\n- Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending.\n- For each node in that order:\n  - if not removable or root or symbol in required_symbols: continue.\n  - Check if any child of this node is in retained_node_ids. (children are those with parent == node.id). If yes, skip.\n  - Check required symbols: compute set of symbols in subtree of this node. Compute symbols that would be lost = those in subtree set. For each required symbol, check if it is present in the overall tree outside this subtree. Overall tree symbols = union of all node symbols. We can precompute total_symbol_set = set(symbol for node in nodes). Then after removal, the remaining symbols = total_symbol_set - lost_symbols + maybe? Actually if a symbol appears only in this subtree and nowhere else, it will be lost. So condition: for every s in required_symbols, s is in total_symbol_set - lost_symbols (or equivalently, s not in lost_symbols or s appears elsewhere). Simpler: lost_symbols is the set of symbols that appear only in the subtree (i.e., appear in subtree and not outside). But easier: for each required symbol, check if there exists any node not in the subtree that has that symbol. We can precompute for each node a set of symbols in its subtree. Then for a given node, we need to know if each required symbol appears in at least one node outside the subtree. We can precompute a symbol_counts map (symbol to count of nodes with that symbol). Then we can compute the count of that symbol inside the subtree. If count inside equals total count, then removal would eliminate it. So compute subtree_symbol_counts (a dict from symbol to count in subtree). For each required symbol s, if total_counts[s] - subtree_counts[s] == 0, then cannot remove. Else ok.\n\n- If conditions satisfied:\n   - Add node.id to removed_node_ids.\n   - Remove node.id from retained_node_ids.\n   - Also remove all descendant nodes from retained_node_ids? But they will be processed later (they are deeper) and they may have already been retained or removed. If they were already removed, they are not in retained_node_ids. If they were retained, they will be processed in this loop? Wait, we are iterating from deepest to shallowest. So for a node that is a descendant, we have already processed it (since deeper). If we have not removed it (i.e., it remained retained), then it is currently in retained_node_ids. When we now remove the parent, those descendant nodes must also be removed. So we need to remove them from retained_node_ids as well. But they are already processed; they will not be considered again (since loop is descending depth). So we must explicitly remove all descendants from retained_node_ids. Also we should update any data structures like symbol_counts? But since we are removing the entire subtree, we can adjust the total symbol counts by subtracting the subtree counts. But we don't need to because we only use total_counts for checking removal conditions of ancestors later. If we subtract subtree counts from total, then later ancestors (shallower) will see updated total. That is correct because the nodes are already removed. So we should maintain a running total symbol counts that reflects currently retained nodes. Actually the check for a node should use the current state of retained nodes, not the original total. Because if some nodes have been removed earlier, the remaining symbols are only from retained nodes. So we need to update the symbol counts as we remove subtrees.\n\nThus better: maintain a current set of retained nodes (initially all). Also maintain current symbol counts (count of nodes with each symbol in retained set). Also maintain for each node the set of symbols in its subtree (based on original tree). Those can be precomputed and are static.\n\nProcessing loop:\n- For each node in descending depth, ascending id:\n  - if node.id not in retained_node_ids: continue (because it may have been removed as descendant of earlier processed node).\n  - if not removable or node symbol in required_symbols or node is root (maybe check parent is null): continue.\n  - Check if any immediate child (by parent field) is in retained_node_ids. (children are those not removed? Actually children might be in retained or not; we need to check if any child is retained. Since we remove descendants as we remove parents, but we are processing deeper first, so if a child was retained but its parent is being considered, the child is already processed and may have been retained. The condition says \"node with retained children\" means cannot remove if any child is still retained. So we check if any child id is in retained_node_ids.)\n  - Compute the set of node ids in the subtree of this node (including itself) that are still retained. Actually the entire subtree might have some nodes removed earlier; but since we process deeper first, if any descendant was removed, it's not in retained, but the node itself and other descendants may be retained. When we remove the current node, we need to remove all retained nodes in its subtree. So we need to compute the set of retained descendants. We can do a DFS to collect all retained nodes in subtree.\n  - For each required symbol s, compute how many nodes with that symbol are in the subtree (among retained nodes). If the count equals the total current count of that symbol (in all retained nodes), then removal would eliminate it -> cannot remove.\n  - If all good:\n    - For each retained node in the subtree (including current node), remove from retained_node_ids and add its id to removed_node_ids (in some order? The output order for removed_node_ids should be the order of actual removal. Since we are processing nodes in depth order, and removing a parent will remove multiple nodes at once. The spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" For removed_node_ids, it should be the order in which nodes were removed. When we remove a parent, we also remove its descendants. Should they appear after the parent in removal order? Typically, in Perses, removal of a node removes its entire subtree, and the node itself is removed first. The order might be parent then descendants but the spec says \"actual removal order\". Possibly the order of removals is the order of decisions, so when we decide to remove a node, we record it, and also all its subtree is removed at that moment. But we could record the parent first, then its descendants in depth order? That might be ambiguous. I think it's safer to record them in the order we process them in the removal operation. We can collect all ids in the subtree, sort them by depth descending (since deeper nodes are removed first? Actually when removing a subtree, the parent is removed, and the children are removed as a consequence. The order might be parent first? But the algorithm processes deeper first so descendants might have been considered earlier. If they were retained, they will now be removed as part of parent removal. I think for simplicity, we can record the current node first, then the descendants (in any order, maybe depth descending). But the spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" It doesn't require sorted for removed. So we can just append them in the order we remove them. Since the algorithm is deterministic given tie-breaking, the order will be deterministic. I'll record the current node id, then recursively collect and remove descendants, appending them after. That reflects that the parent removal triggers removal of children.\n\n- After loop, retained_node_ids is the set of nodes not removed.\n- Sort retained_node_ids lexicographically (since they are strings). The spec says \"sorted retained IDs\" - likely alphabetical.\n- removed_node_ids list as built.\n\nEdge cases: required_symbols may include symbols that are not present in any node? Then problem is infeasible? Raise ValueError? The spec says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So if required_symbols includes a symbol not present in any node, that's infeasible. Also if root is required? Root is always present. But we need to check at start.\n\nAlso, we must never remove the root node. Root has parent null and removable false. So fine.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Validate case has 'nodes' list and 'required_symbols' list. Each node has required fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. (utility not used but present.)\n2. Build a dictionary node_by_id.\n3. Build adjacency list for children.\n4. Check that root exists (parent null). Could have multiple roots? Assume one.\n5. Precompute total_symbol_counts from all nodes.\n6. Precompute subtree_symbol_counts for each node using DFS post-order (including itself). Use recursion or stack.\n7. Initialize retained_node_ids = set of all node ids.\n8. Initialize removed = [].\n9. Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending. We'll iterate over this list, but we need to skip nodes not in retained.\n10. For each node in sorted order:\n   - if node.id not in retained: continue.\n   - if not node['removable']: continue.\n   - if node['symbol'] in required_symbols: continue.\n   - if node['parent'] is None: continue (root).\n   - check children: for child in children[node.id]: if child.id in retained: skip this node (cannot remove).\n   - Now compute subtree_members: all retained nodes in the subtree. We can do a DFS limited to retained nodes. Since we have precomputed subtree ids? We can do a recursive traversal of children. But careful: the subtree might have been partially removed already. We only care about retained nodes. We'll collect all retained node ids in the subtree (including current node). Also compute counts of symbols in that subtree among retained nodes.\n   - For each required symbol s, if current_total_counts[s] - subtree_counts.get(s,0) == 0: cannot remove.\n   - If all passed:\n     - Add node.id to removed.\n     - Remove node.id from retained, update total_counts.\n     - For each descendant retained node (in the subtree_members excluding current node), we need to remove them as well. We can iterate over subtree_members (excluding current) in any order. For each, remove from retained, add to removed, update total_counts.\n     - After removal, continue loop.\n\nBut note: when we remove the current node and its subtree, we also need to update the parent-child relationships? Not needed.\n\nComplexities: O(N^2) if we recompute subtree members every time. But N is small (in fixtures, 7 nodes). Acceptable.\n\nWe must ensure that we do not rely on hash ordering. Use sorted iteration.\n\nAlso, we must not mutate the input case.\n\nReturn dict with keys removed_node_ids (list) and retained_node_ids (sorted list).\n\nTesting on first fixture:\nNodes:\n root (depth0, removable false)\n n1 (depth1, removable false, symbol s1)\n n2 (depth1, removable true, symbol s2)\n n3 (depth1, removable false, symbol s0)\n n4 (depth2, parent n2, removable true, symbol s1)\n n5 (depth2, parent n3, removable false, symbol s2)\n n6 (depth2, parent n1, removable true, symbol s0)\nrequired: root, s1.\n\nProcessing deepest first: nodes at depth2: n4, n5, n6. Order by id: n4, n5, n6.\nn4: removable true, symbol s1. But s1 is required, so cannot remove (since required symbol). So skip.\nn5: removable false, skip.\nn6: removable true, symbol s0 not in required, parent n1 (present). Check children: none. Check required symbols: subtree symbols of n6 = {s0}. total symbols currently: root,s1,s2,s0 (n6 has s0, n3 has s0, n1 has s1, root has root,...). Required s1: total count of s1 = nodes: n1, n4. Subtree of n6 has no s1, so removal fine. Required root: root has symbol root, not in subtree. So can remove. So we remove n6. removed = [n6]. Remove from retained. Update total_counts: decrement s0 count.\nNow depth2 nodes done. Depth1 nodes: n1, n2, n3. Order by id: n1, n2, n3.\nn1: removable false, skip.\nn2: removable true, symbol s2 not required. Children: n4 (still retained). So has retained child -> cannot remove.\nn3: removable false, skip.\nDepth0: root: removable false skip.\n\nFinal retained: all except n6. removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"]. retained sorted: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]. That matches some expectation? But we need to check second condition: never remove a required symbol. That's fine. But what about n4? It has required symbol s1, so cannot be removed. OK.\n\nSecond fixture similar.\n\nBut there might be cases where multiple nodes can be removed in sequence. For example, if n2 had no children and not required, then it could be removed. Then later its parent? root is not removable.\n\nThe algorithm seems correct.\n\nNow implement solve(case) accordingly.\n\nPotential pitfalls: \n- The \"removable\" field is boolean. In fixtures it's True/False.\n- The \"parent\" field is string or null for root.\n- The \"depth\" field is integer.\n- The \"id\" field is string.\n\nWe need to handle missing fields? Spec says validate every required field before computing. So if any required field missing, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso, the spec says \"grammar_valid\", \"interestingness_preserved\" but we treat required_symbols as the interestingness oracle.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate inputs\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"case must have 'nodes' and 'required_symbols'\")\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or len(nodes) == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"nodes must be non-empty list\")\n    # validate each node\n    required_node_fields = ['id','parent','symbol','depth','removable']\n    for n in nodes:\n        for f in required_node_fields:\n            if f not in n:\n                raise ValueError(f\"node missing field {f}\")\n    # build structures\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    # check root\n    roots = [n for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None]\n    if len(roots) != 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"must have exactly one root\")\n    root_id = roots[0]['id']\n    # children map\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_by_id}\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            if pid not in node_by_id:\n                raise ValueError(f\"parent {pid} not found\")\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\n    # precompute subtree symbol sets? Actually we need counts for required symbols check.\n    # We'll compute subtree symbol counts (count of each symbol in subtree) for each node based on original tree.\n    # But later we need counts of retained symbols only. We'll compute on the fly using retained set.\n    # Alternatively, we can precompute full subtree members and symbol counts, then adjust when removing.\n    # Simpler: for each node, compute set of subtree node ids (full tree). Then when checking removal, we can compute retained subset by intersecting with retained set.\n    subtree_node_ids = {}\n    def dfs_subtree(nid):\n        members = {nid}\n        for c in children[nid]:\n            members.update(dfs_subtree(c))\n        subtree_node_ids[nid] = members\n        return members\n    dfs_subtree(root_id)\n    # Also precompute full subtree symbol counts for each node? Not needed if we compute from retained.\n    \n    # Initial retained set and counts\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\n    total_counts = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        s = n['symbol']\n        total_counts[s] = total_counts.get(s, 0) + 1\n    \n    # Check required symbols exist\n    for s in required_symbols:\n        if s not in total_counts:\n            raise ValueError(f\"required symbol {s} not present\")\n    \n    removed = []  # list in order\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        if node['parent'] is None:\n            continue  # root\n        # Check retained children\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Compute retained nodes in subtree\n        subtree_set = subtree_node_ids[nid]  # all nodes in full subtree\n        retained_subset = subtree_set & retained\n        if not retained_subset:\n            continue  # should not happen as node itself is retained\n        # Compute symbol counts in retained_subset\n        sub_counts = {}\n        for rid in retained_subset:\n            s = node_by_id[rid]['symbol']\n            sub_counts[s] = sub_counts.get(s, 0) + 1\n        # Check required symbols\n        can_remove = True\n        for s in required_symbols:\n            if total_counts.get(s,0) - sub_counts.get(s,0) == 0:\n                can_remove = False\n                break\n        if not can_remove:\n            continue\n        # Remove all retained nodes in subtree\n        # Order: first the node itself, then its descendants (deterministic order: depth descending then id)\n        # We'll collect and sort internally for deterministic removal order.\n        removal_list = sorted(retained_subset, key=lambda rid: (-node_by_id[rid]['depth'], rid))\n        for rid in removal_list:\n            removed.append(rid)\n            retained.remove(rid)\n            s = node_by_id[rid]['symbol']\n            total_counts[s] -= 1\n            if total_counts[s] == 0:\n                del total_counts[s]  # optional\n    # End loop\n    # Sort retained\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n```\n\nCheck with first fixture: We already reasoned removal of n6 only. The algorithm: n6 processed, removed; then n4 cannot because required; n2 has retained child n4; so final retained includes n1,n2,n3,n4,n5,root. removed = [\"n6\"]. That matches.\n\nSecond fixture: same pattern.\n\nThird: similar.\n\nNow ensure no mutation: we are only reading from case.\n\nPotential edge: Node with no children and not removable? Skipped.\n\nIf a node is removable but its subtree contains required symbols, we skip.\n\nNow we must output implementation as a JSON string. The response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value the Python source as a string, escaped.\n\nI'll produce that.\n\nBut also note: The spec says \"Do not rename fields or change their nesting.\" That refers to output schema; we are returning the specified dict.\n\nMake sure to include only standard library.\n\nI'll write the code now.\n\nPotential improvements: For performance, but not needed.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":6871,"total_tokens":9040,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5958},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":377},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}